* text=auto eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
# Retained publisher bodies are EXACT BYTES. Their digests are asserted in tests and one is even
# its own filename, so a single rewritten line ending makes a fixture that is not the bytes it
# names, and every digest assertion against it silently wrong. The "* text=auto eol=lf" default
# above is wrong for them: git resolves .bin as "text: auto", so it applies its own content
# heuristic per file rather than any declaration here. Of the twelve tracked .bin files, eight are
# NUL-free and so are seen as TEXT with the eol filter running over them, surviving only because
# they happen to contain no CRLF at all; the other four are seen as binary only because they
# contain NUL, and one of those carries 288 CRLF that would be rewritten without it. Safety was
# therefore a property of each file's bytes rather than of any rule. Declaring it makes the next
# NUL-free XHTML or XML body safe by construction. Adding this changes no stored blob: all twelve
# already round trip identically, so it is prevention, not repair.
# The incident that proved this: staging the retained Akoma Ntoso body without a binary
# declaration rewrote its CRLF and changed its digest from 9e43a99e.. to fc3303d4.., which
# would have committed a fixture that is not the publisher bytes it names. That was first
# fixed with a lane-scoped path rule; this general *.bin rule supersedes it, so the scoped
# line is gone and one rule covers every publisher body.
*.bin binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
